// MINIMAL CRASHING repro — bin/souc SRET-forward in an aggregate → SIGSEGV (2026-06-02)
//
// Forwarding a struct-returning call's result INSIDE a tuple (aggregate) escalates the
// SRET-forwarding zeroing bug to a hard crash (rc=139). 4 lines, deterministic.
//
// Expected: prints 7.000000.   Actual: SIGSEGV (exit 139), no output.
// Compile+run: ./bin/souc this.sio /tmp/c.elf && chmod +x /tmp/c.elf && /tmp/c.elf; echo $?
//
// Relation to the G1 lane's "SRET-smash" / "large-struct return-value miscompile" crash
// (CRASH_CLASS_ZERO / E008_ROOT_CAUSE docs): SAME named family. Differences (honest):
//   - This crash is DETERMINISTIC (changing the `1` to `2` still crashes identically);
//     the G1 arg-checker crash "relocates per source change" (layout-sensitive).
//   - This is the FORWARDING instance (return another call's struct); theirs is the
//     arg-checker copy of a 272-byte TypeEntry on `g(5)`-style calls-with-args.
//   So: a clean minimal sibling in the family they named — a deterministic test case for
//   the codegen fix — NOT proven byte-identical to their relocating crash.

struct S { f0: f64 }
fn ctor() -> S with Mut { var p: S = S { f0: 7.0 }; return p }
fn make() -> (S, i64) with Mut { return (ctor(), 1) }   // forward-in-aggregate -> SIGSEGV
fn main() with IO, Mut { let t = make(); print_f64(t.0.f0); print("\n") }
